     "up": {"title":"The missing element (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A team pitches a Caribbean history game. It opens with a vision, gives a striking problem, demonstrates the game, describes the target market, names two competitors, introduces the team and ends: \"We are asking for USD 10,000 from your fund.\" The panel declines, saying: \"We do not know whether anyone wants this, or what our money would achieve.\" Which change would MOST directly answer the panel's concerns?","options":[{"t":"Lower the ask to USD 5,000.","fb":"A smaller ask still gives no evidence of demand and no purpose for the money."},{"t":"Add a longer introduction and more slides about the history behind the game.","fb":"More background does not show demand or explain how the money would be used."},{"t":"Remove the competition section so the game seems unique.","fb":"Claiming no competition makes panels less confident, and it does not address either concern."},{"t":"Add traction, such as pilot players or pre-orders, and expand the ask to say what the USD 10,000 will fund and what the funder gains.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Traction answers \"does anyone want this?\", and a full ask answers \"what would our money achieve?\"."}]}
