     "up": {"title":"The sprint (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A Lower Six team is producing a podcast in four two-week sprints. Halfway through Sprint 2, the client asks for a new episode on Carnival history. Which response follows agile sprint practice BEST?","options":[{"t":"Extend Sprint 2 to six weeks so that everything fits.","fb":"Sprints are short and time-boxed (one to four weeks). Stretching them delays review and feedback."},{"t":"Refuse, because the plan was fixed in the planning phase.","fb":"Refusing all change is waterfall thinking."},{"t":"Stop Sprint 2 at once and start the Carnival episode, because agile welcomes change.","fb":"Agile welcomes change between sprints, but a sprint is time-boxed; abandoning committed work mid-sprint wastes it."},{"t":"Add the Carnival episode to the product backlog, finish the work committed for Sprint 2, then set its priority when planning the next sprint after review.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The change is welcomed, but it enters through the backlog so the time-boxed sprint stays focused."}]}
